Jove wrote:
>   And there's *nothing* wrong with just playing the parts of a
> game you like.

There a part of the game that most players don't like.  That's a
weakness, as the entire point of a game is to be fun.  Making that part
of the game more enjoyable would make it a better game overall, for
most players.

The fact that some people resort to quitting early is a deficiency in
the game.  The fact that some people "put up with" Gehennom to get to
the next fun part is a deficiency in the game.

Heck, read most YAAPS.  Long description of events up to the castle.
Then "Gehennom was the usual slog..blah blah blah...Orcustown...blah
blah blah...Vlad was a pushover, found the VS".  Then back to
description starting with Rodney.  You see some commentary on the
unique levels (maybe Juiblex, Asmodeus, Baalzebub will make an
appearance too) but otherwise pretty much nothing unless Demogorgon or
Dispater somehow shows up.  Contrast to the main dungeon where the
levels are more differentiated and there are challenges to the player
(found a shop, anthill, leprechaun hall, etc; attacked by a swarm of
rothes and had to do X,Y, or Z; whatever it is, you see a lot more
highlights of what happened here).

>   Beating most commercial games means you read the walk through.
>
>   Beating Nethack means "You ascend to the status of demi-god."

Sure.  And making the middle-game more challenging, and therefore more
fun, might even make it _harder_ to ascend, and therefore more
meaningful.

Right now, Gehennom is a chore that a lot of people endure because they
get to play the (much more rarely seen than the starting) end-game and
are working toward a fun goal.  But eliminating that chore and
replacing it with even higher fun value would be a relief to many.

Regardless, this line of argument is basically fruitless.  It seems
that there is a general consensus from the group that the game would be
improved by altering or shortening the Gehennom section.  It's not a
new sentiment (people were disgruntled long before the Heck patches).
I'd much rather discuss ways of improving those parts than try to
convince a handful of outliers as to the value of that--especially
since those outliers aren't necessarily wrong, if you enjoy grinding
then Gehennom could be cathartic.

Basically I think we just disagree on the goal of a game and/or on what
is fun, and there's nothing wrong with that but we're not going to
convince each other.



